</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Ablation Study on Translator Quility</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To isolate the effect of the NL2DSL translator from the DPO reinforcement learning signal, we conduct an ablation study in which we train a weaker translator, using less data, and thus achieving lower performance. As shown in Table <a href="#A5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ E.2 Ablation Study on Translator Quility ‣ Appendix E More Experiments ‣ Enhancing Geometric Perception in VLMs via Translator-Guided Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and Table <a href="#A5.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ E.2 Ablation Study on Translator Quility ‣ Appendix E More Experiments ‣ Enhancing Geometric Perception in VLMs via Translator-Guided Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, the performance of the GeoDPO framework significantly degrades when the quality of the translator is compromised. This confirms that the quality of the translator directly influences the effectiveness of the reward signal in our method.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T11" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span>Performance of Translator Trained on Different Amounts of Data.</figcaption>
